  "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{4x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}{e}^{4x}+8{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{4x}+16u{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{\u2033}{e}^{4x}+8{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{4x}+16u{e}^{4x})-3({u}^{\u2032}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x})-4u{e}^{4x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4x}({u}^{\u2033}+8{u}^{\u2032}+16u-3{u}^{\u2032}-12u-4u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4x}({u}^{\u2033}+5{u}^{\u2032}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4x}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2033}+5{u}^{\u2032}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{\u2032}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{\u2032}=-5w.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{\u2032},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}={c}_{1}{e}^{-5x}\\,\u27f9\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{4x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5}{e}^{-5x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-5x}={e}^{-x}.\n$$",
